after_row('P53', "| P54 | Height-uniform pointwise or windowed inequality in the values of finitely many Dirichlet L-functions in 1/2 < Re s < 1, as a step claimed to carry arithmetic (filter UNIV) | Relative to joint (and hybrid) universality: a sup-norm-closed condition with height-independent constants on a fixed compact window with connected complement, holding outside a set of heights of upper density zero, holds for every non-vanishing analytic tuple on the window (Lemma U1); a continuous inequality in finitely many values and derivatives at fixed offsets then holds on the whole jet space (no content). Three classes: T (true for every analytic tuple), N (true for every non-vanishing tuple; automatic on zero-free windows and able to fail only on a window containing a zero; the sentence \"equal in content to a zero statement\" holds in that sense only, referee E2), F (false on a set of heights of positive lower density). Linear combinations of two or more of the L-functions with non-vanishing Dirichlet-polynomial coefficients have at least cT zeros in every sub-strip (Kaczorowski-Kulas, as printed in the survey). Not covered: Re s <= 1/2; Re s > 1 (control U); statements non-local in height (t with 2t, positive-definiteness in t, mean values, sums over zeros); families growing with T and the conductor aspect; inequalities using s together with 1 - conj s or the completed function; height-dependent constants or regions; existence in short intervals; companions that are not Dirichlet L-functions. Not numerically checkable (heights not located; a sample to height 8000 shows 58 of 256 sign patterns). Against 34 device rows: decides 1 (device 7, a restatement by Bagchi), part of 4 (1, 4, 14, 25), restates 2 (12, 32), out of scope for 27. | PROVED-rel joint universality as printed in Matsumoto arXiv:1407.4216 (Theorems 7, 10, 11, 20, 30, 31 opened by the lane and by its referee; primary texts not opened); U2 and U5 are CLASSICAL consequences, not new; device codes ARGUED; one independent referee, five corrections | the family of non-vanishing analytic tuples, realised by height translates of the L-functions themselves (the filter is zeta's own value distribution, not a foreign function) | `c12-univ-pointwise-control-wcone` (+ REFEREE.md, independent pass; `DEVICE_READ_UNIV.md`) |")
after_row('G32', "| G33 | Hecke operators T_{p^2}, U_2 and the Fricke involution w_4 acting on theta restricted to the real oval of X_0(4), as a base-side loading of the arc (classical identities; bookkeeping of real against complex isogenous points) | Mellin transform along the arc turns every term into a Dirichlet polynomial in p^(-s) times Lambda(s). T_{p^2} has 2 real and p^2 + p - 2 complex isogenous points (outside a countable set of x); real points: multiplier R_p(s) = 2 p^(-3/2) cosh((s - 1/2) log p); summed complex points: C_p = 1 + 1/p - R_p, a combination of dilates of theta, positive on the arc (provable from monotonicity of theta, referee remark 1). R_p + C_p = 1 + 1/p is the relation c(pm) = c(m), the Euler factor: an identity. C_p Lambda is -p^(-3/2) times the T-NR twin with c = -(p^(3/2) + p^(1/2)), whose extra zeros lie outside the closed strip; every twin, including those with 2 < abs(c) < q^(1/2) + q^(-1/2), is (q^(-1/2) D_{q^-2} + q^(1/2) D_{q^2} + c) theta and lies in the module generated by dilations on theta, so no statement about the module separates them (referee E2). <D_l, W> acts on a W-fixed vector through the commutative quotient a + bW -> a + b. Termwise bounds lose (p - 1) p^(-1/2). Unsummed complex points: a single point has a Mellin transform with zeros off the line (measured, p = 5); the second moment over the points tau + b/p is Theta_{L+} + Theta_{L-} - theta(p^2 x)^2, row G16, with off-line zeros for the class [2,2,5] (measured). Closed for four forms: linear expressions in Hecke images of theta on the arc, dilation-covariant quadratic forms, single complex points, their second moment. Not excluded: a functional of the unsummed complex points that is neither linear nor a second moment (no candidate on file). | PROVED (Lemmas 1 to 4, elementary; identities classical); MEASURED (off-line zeros, float, two methods, not enclosed); one independent referee, four corrections | T-NR (every abs(c) > 2) and E_a with real a pass every step; Epstein classes n = 5, 6 pass positivity and Fricke and fail only at the identity step; DH fails multiplicativity at p; backward heat ARGUED (not formulable off the arc); Beurling, phase-rotated transfer operator, theta^w not run | `c12-x04-oval-hecke-fricke-loading` (+ REFEREE.md, independent pass) |")
after_row('Q28', "| Q29 | Cone adapted to the functional-equation matrix: K_F = {v >= 0, Fv >= 0} on even functions on Z/q with the dilations as cone automorphisms; candidate (CW) (the cell \"a cone other than R_+^G\" left open by row Q28) | Linear inequalities of K_F are the entrywise ones at x and 1/x (Lemma C). Every dilation-invariant pointed cone meets the dilation eigenvectors only in the trivial slice (Lemma P', with the one-line step supplied by the referee, E3); that a copositive form over such a cone contains the scalar zeta inequality with the cone inert is ARGUED. At q = 5 the F-fixed extreme rays give zeta(s)(1 + 5^(1/2-s)) +- L(s, chi_5): positive kernel, zeta's functional equation, at least cT zeros in every sub-strip (relative to the survey's Theorem 31) and measured zeros off the line (0.7796 + 61.1685i and five more below 120). The T-NR lift has every input of (CW) (kernel in K_F with the same F, coefficient matrices cone endomorphisms); its zeros are inside the strip only for 2 < c < 2.1213 at p_0 = 2 (the lane's 2.5 corrected, E1). The E_a lift fails only at the polar terms of the theta-normalised kernel (E*(i, 0.7) = -2.331). Automorphism at every prime is an input that no cone inequality consumes (cone inequalities are preserved by cone endomorphisms); projective contraction is void (two Perron vectors). No value-side cone in the open strip (row P54). Closed: candidate (CW) on K_F. Not covered: other F-adapted cones (non-polyhedral, or not containing v_+-). | PROVED (Lemmas C, P'); COMPUTED (Lemma X at q = 5); PROVED-rel (Lemma Z, rel. the survey); kernels and zeros MEASURED; one independent referee, five corrections | T-NR lift passes every step; DH-type extreme rays pass positivity, the functional equation and the cone; E_a fails at the polar terms of the kernel; THIN, U, DEL fail at the functional equation | `c12-univ-pointwise-control-wcone` section 4 to 6 (+ REFEREE.md, independent pass) |")
after_row('Z34', "| Z35 | Depth-aspect termwise exchange: replace the height twist n^(-it) by a character mod p^k on n <= N, so that a zero at height t is posed in a periodic family with the functional equation of the target used at the exchange | The termwise exchange step (E) is false: no character mod p^k is within 0.479/s of c n^(-it) on n <= N once N >= C_r p (p^k abs(t))^(1/r) and abs(t) >= t_r, for each r >= 2 (Lemma X; s = r for r <= 5; thresholds t_r = 40.3, 3.4e5, 7.7e7, 2.3e10 for r = 2..5). For an r-datum, prod (m + a_i H) = prod (m + b_i H) mod H^r (Lemma P) while the real ratio is 1 +- kappa (H/m)^r (1 + o(1)): integers below N supply multiplicative relations that are trivial p-adically and non-trivial at the real place. Covers N >= sqrt(q)(sqrt(t) + 1) for even k and t >= 41 (r = 2; odd k: sqrt(pq)(sqrt(t/1.12) + 1)), and N >= max(sqrt(q), sqrt(t/2 pi)) for abs(t) >= 2.27e10, p^k abs(t) >= (9p)^20 (r = 5). What is refuted is sup-norm termwise agreement of the twists, a sufficient condition for the transfer; a transfer through weighted sums special to the coefficients of zeta is neither found nor excluded (referee G1). Bounded heights with k -> infinity are covered by the written lemma only for r = 2 and abs(t) >= 41; at t = 14.134725 the conclusion holds in the cases tried (exact check at q = 3^30, 5^21, 2^60 by the referee; not a written proof, G2). An exchange is measured to exist only up to N of about 11 to 17 at log q between 6.4 and 12.1 for abs(t) >= 14 (Lemma D gives existence without rate; for small abs(t) the principal character is an exchange at every N, G3). In the 7/8 format the depth characters are already targets (no slot stores a height) and as a row family they are a full dual group (Parseval, circular). Height and depth are independent coordinates of the dual of R_{>0} x Z_p^x; the analytic conductor p^k (1 + abs(t)) is a product. The depth aspect as an analogue of the t-aspect is standard (Postnikov, Gallagher, Milicevic, Blomer-Milicevic; not cited by the lane); Lemma X is a correct elementary lemma, not presented as new (G6). | PROVED (Lemmas P, X; elementary; re-derived by one independent referee, exact arithmetic and brute force); CLASSICAL (Lemma D); MEASURED (six moduli); READ (format requirements); five overstatements narrowed by the referee | none separated: (E) reads only the two twists, so zeta, U, DH, E_a and T-NR fail it identically | `c12-depth-aspect-height-transfer` (+ REFEREE.md, independent pass; `referee-general/`) |")
after_row('D7', "| D8 | Positivity in the Bost-Connes algebra C*(Q/Z) x N^x (isometries mu_n, phases e(r), non-commuting) joined to the Fourier duality (campaign round 12) | (i) Every sigma-invariant state factors through the conditional expectation onto C*(Q/Z) = C(Z^). For the Galois-invariant KMS_beta state the measure is a product, its values on the algebraic Hecke algebra are entire in beta and built from finitely many primes; for the extremal KMS states at beta > 1 the values are combinations of L(beta, chi)/zeta(beta) (meromorphic in beta, all primes; referee E1), and positivity there is positivity of the coefficients k^-beta at real beta > 1, passed by every control with non-negative coefficients. (ii) The Weil prime side is phi_{1/2}(H_g), H_g = sum Lambda(n) g(log n) mu_n mu_n^*; Weil's criterion is an upper bound for this state value on a non-positive element (row P1). (iii) For the archimedean vector states sum f(lambda k) e_k the Mellin transform of every matrix coefficient is a periodic zeta function times an archimedean factor: read set {L(s, chi)}; the mu_n step and the Poisson step are identities and the inequality is a square. (iv) Bochner positivity of the state holds on lines sigma > 1 and is lost at the pole; a sign-reversed kernel positivity holds on every line 0 < sigma < 1 for monotone F (Muntz formula; classical) and carries no zero information (referee). (v) T-NR is beta -> Tr(D e^{-beta H}) with D >= 1 a fixed positive element of C*(Q/Z) in the centraliser; it differs from zeta only in exact mu_q covariance at the one prime q. E_a is the same device on A (x) A. DEL with finite S passes the mu_n step and the Poisson step exactly (referee, control table). Closed for three classes of states: sigma-invariant states, the dual-system state, Mellin families of archimedean vector states. Not covered: other states (type II_infinity weights at beta <= 1; positivity there is the Weil form). Correction to ROUND_LOG round 10: KMS_beta exists for every beta > 0; the read set \"finitely many local factors\" is that of the Galois-invariant state, and \"real beta > 1\" is the correct read set of the extremal states. | PROVED (Lemmas C first half, V, T as corrected, B proved part, W; identities checked at 30 digits by the lane and with other cases by its referee); ARGUED or classical-not-opened (Lemma P last sentence, Lemma B last sentence; Bost-Connes 1995, Takesaki, Connes 1999, Connes-Marcolli 2004 not opened); one independent referee, six corrections | E_a, T-NR (c > 2; zero inside the strip only for c < q^(1/2) + q^(-1/2)), DEL with finite S pass every step; window translate (the gauge action, not an independent control); EIS-w argued | `c12-bost-connes-crossed-product-duality` (+ REFEREE.md, independent pass; `MAP_ROW.md`) |")

